{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>Blot me out, I pray, from Your book.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this is the Book of the Torah, and he meant that he should not be mentioned in the Torah and that it should not be given through him. <b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'from Your book' — the curse of a sage, even if conditional, comes to pass. From where do we learn this? From Moses, who said 'blot me out' — and even though the sin of the Calf was atoned for them, his name was blotted out from the parashah of ואתה תצוה, where the name of Moses is not mentioned at all." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): 'Your book' refers to the arrangement of the stars — the book of the Holy One, blessed be He. For just as a book is an engraving and depiction of letters concerning matters of wisdom, so too in the arrangement of the stars there is engraving and depiction of forces concerning the affairs of man and the number of the days of their lives in this world, recorded and engraved there like a thing engraved and written in a book. And so David said (Psalms 139:16): ״גלמי ראו עיניך ועל ספרך כלם יכתבו״." "<b>And behold,</b> the force of a man which is above is the foundation of his existence and permanence below, and the blotting out of the force is its annihilation, which is his death. And therefore Moses asked in supplication from Him, blessed be He, that if He would not bear their sin, He should blot out his force from the arrangement of the stars — that is, that He should kill him, for he could not bear to see their evil. And this is what is written elsewhere (Numbers 11:15): ״הרגני נא הרג אם מצאתי חן בעיניך״, and this is the language of the righteous, who suffer and grieve over their lives. And so Elijah said (I Kings 19:4): ״קח נפשי כי לא טוב אנכי מאבותי״, and so Jonah son of Amittai said (Jonah 4:3): ״קח נא את נפשי ממני כי טוב מותי מחיי״. And what he added, ״אשר כתבת״ — 'which You have written' — the intention is that which You created anew, for that which is newly made testifies to the One Who renews it, just as writing testifies to the writer. And Moses intended to say by this that since He, blessed be He, created those forces anew, it is in His power to annul them. And the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: ״מי אשר חטא לי אמחנו מספרי״ — because of the greatness of His wrath against them, He mentioned them in an indefinite manner and did not wish to mention them by name. And He said to Moses: You are not fit for this punishment, for you did not sin in this at all; but 'whoever has sinned against Me' — he is the one fit to be blotted out from My book, that is, I shall annul their force from the arrangement, and from this they shall be annulled from the world. And in this manner He mentioned above: ״וחר אפי בהם ואכלם״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'Your book' — from the sefirot. And it is as if He said: from the six extremities above, which are six sefirot, for they are the beginning of every operation and they are called the work of heaven and earth. And this is the secret of the verse (Psalms 69:29): ״ימחו מספר חיים״ — from the language of sefirot as well, and it is as if He said: from the bond of life." "<b>And from here</b> you should understand the statement of the masters of truth: three books are opened on Rosh HaShanah — one for the wholly righteous, one for the wholly wicked, and one for the intermediate. And the intention is that the three books are חסד, דין, and רחמים (Hesed, Din, Raḥamim). The wholly righteous — their cause is Hesed; the wholly wicked — their cause is Din; the intermediate — their cause is Raḥamim, which decides between the two. And the King whom we mention on Rosh HaShanah acts through all three of them."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:44.625428211Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}